# acc.add_product(x, y) of Accumulator<T, 1> (milestone2.md, "The accumulator"): acc_mul_1 on x and
# y guarded (rule R3, the factors of a fused operation: architecture.md). acc_mul_1 only multiplies
# x and y, which R3 alone does not guard; but a compiler that sees one of them to be 1 rewrites
# fma(x, 1, s) as x + s and fuses a product x = a b of the program into that addition (Apple clang
# 21 under -ffp-contract=fast, measured). After the guards each is a number of the format. Its
# claims are those of acc_mul_1: the guard is the identity on numbers of the format.
kernel:        acc_mul_entry_1
input:         stored s
input:         stored a
input:         x
input:         y
output:        t
output:        b
value:         s+x*y
requires:      nonneg a
ensures:       rounded t nearest
ensures:       rounded b nearest value=a+abs(x)*abs(y)
theorem:       proofs/theories/AccKernels.v acc_mul_entry_1_correct
ieee_spec_theorem: proofs/theories/IeeeAccKernels.v acc_mul_entry_1_ieee_correct
min_precision: 1
source:        acc_mul_1 on the guarded x and y (rule R3, the factors of a fused operation)
license:       proof: MIT
status:        proved
ieee:          spec
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   b, the sum of magnitudes, does not reach t: its claim is read where b is finite (an entry about an output group other than the first), and where b is +inf the mask ok of acc_radius_k is false

gx = guard(x)
gy = guard(y)
t  = fma(gx, gy, s)
ax = abs(gx)
ay = abs(gy)
b  = fma(ax, ay, a)
